<Base title={siteConfig.name} type="Home">
 <section class="hero"><div><p class="eyebrow">A field guide to acquisition entrepreneurship</p><h1>Buy an existing business.<br/>Build from there.</h1><p class="intro">Starting a company from scratch is one route to entrepreneurship. Buying an existing profitable business is another: start with customers, employees, revenue and operating history, then take responsibility for what comes next.</p><p>This changes the risks; it does not remove them. Existing profit needs validation, and purchase price, financing and the ownership transition can change the outcome.</p><p class="coverage-note spaced">For aspiring acquisition entrepreneurs and first-time buyers, operators considering growth by acquisition, and finance professionals learning practical business-acquisition mechanics.</p><div class="actions"><a class="button" href="/start-here/">New to acquisitions? Start here</a><a href="/course/">Explore the free course →</a></div></div><aside class="hero-aside"><h2>Choose your starting point</h2><ol><li><div><a href="/course/">Learn in sequence</a><p>Fourteen modules, from first principles to owning a group.</p></div></li><li><div><a href="/financing/">Study acquisition finance</a><p>Understand debt, equity, seller terms, and their trade-offs.</p></div></li><li><div><a href="/topics/">Find a specific concept</a><p>Clear definitions, worked examples, and further reading.</p></div></li></ol></aside></section>
 <div class="stats-line"><span><strong>20</strong> course lessons</span><span><strong>44</strong> topic guides</span><span><strong>{videoCounts.total}</strong> source videos</span><span><strong>Free course</strong> no account needed</span></div>
 <section class="section" style="border-top:0"><div class="section-head"><h2>Three questions before making an offer.</h2><a href="/topics/">Browse every topic →</a></div><div class="feature-grid"><article><p class="eyebrow">01 / The business</p><h3>What are you buying?</h3><p>Separate the company’s operating story from the seller’s pitch. Look at customers, management, earnings quality, and the work the owner actually does.</p><a href="/course/choosing-a-target/">Define a useful target →</a></article><article><p class="eyebrow">02 / The economics</p><h3>How much cash is available?</h3><p>Work from earnings to cash, then consider valuation and the timing of repayments. Keep a worked illustration separate from a lender’s actual terms.</p><a href="/course/earnings-versus-cash/">Understand the numbers →</a></article><article><p class="eyebrow">03 / The capital</p><h3>Who funds the acquisition?</h3><p>Compare the roles of a bank, equity partner, seller, or institutional lender. Understand what each contributes and what each expects in return.</p><a href="/course/building-the-capital-stack/">Read the capital stack →</a></article></div><p class="spaced">Continue through <a href="/course/diligencing-the-business/">diligence</a>, <a href="/course/structuring-the-price/">deal structure</a> and <a href="/course/getting-to-completion/">closing</a>, then <a href="/course/management-after-acquisition/">plan for ownership after the purchase</a>.</p></section>
 <section class="section split"><div><p class="eyebrow">A closer look at financing</p><h2>Build a capital stack the business can carry.</h2><p class="spaced">See how buyer equity, bank debt, seller financing and other capital sources fit together — and what the business actually has to support after closing.</p><div class="actions spaced"><a class="button secondary" href="/financing/">Explore acquisition financing →</a></div></div><div class="article-aside"><h2>Put examples in context</h2><p>A rate can be an illustration. A large capital amount can be a target. A promising lender conversation can remain a proposal.</p><ul class="content-list spaced"><li><a href="/examples/">Worked acquisition examples →</a></li><li><a href="/numbers/">Quantitative Reference →</a></li><li><a href="/providers/">Firms and intermediaries →</a></li></ul></div></section>
     <section class="section split"><div><p class="eyebrow">Read beyond the guide</p><h2>A companion to the source material.</h2></div><div><p>Our first collection brings together education from {siteConfig.collections['yusufa-sey'].name}. Acquisition Companion is independently authored: creators are sources, not collaborators, and source attribution does not imply endorsement. This site adds original explanations, comparisons, and learning paths. The videos remain the primary source.</p><p>Machine transcripts can contain errors. We flag incomplete evidence and link you back to the original material rather than presenting every practitioner statement as a settled fact.</p><div class="actions spaced"><a href="/sources/yusufa-sey/">Explore the source collection →</a><a href="/methodology/">How the material was developed</a></div></div></section>
</Base>
